im gonna try to summarise the whole refreshToken part. So the problem.
The problem was that when the accessToken expired the user was forced to login again, mere inconvenience.
The other problem was the security. Either you had to have accessTokens that lasted way to long posing a security danger if that token got stolen,
meaning that the attacker would have access longer. On the other hand, if the accessToken was to short, it would be an inconvenience for users being forced to login constantly.
Solution, separate the auth into 2 parts. A refreshToken and a accessToken. The refreshToken will act as a validator, it doesnt give authorization,
but rather approved the user for a accessToken. So the refreshToken allowed the user to continually get more accessTokens automatically.
This meant that accessTokens could be shorter, mitigating the security risk by a lot, while the refreshToken could hold longer,
essentially removing the security risk and inconvenience. So, what did we to in the backend? We modified so that we send out 2 tokens,
the accessToken and refreshToken on login or register. We created a table for storing RefreshTokens. But why store them?
when we dont store accessTokens. We want to store refreshToken because we need a way to revoke them as they are so long lasting.
Without having them server-side someone could get a refreshToken and use it as they please to get more accessTokens until expire.
Further created logic for the refreshToken, means to revoke and remove them from DB. For the frontend, we updated the fetch methods,
types and context to hold refreshToken in local storage. For the big part: the queue. We don't want users to need to login after accessToken goes out,
we want to send a refreshRequest automatically, otherwise the entire purpose of the refreshTokens would be redundant, it needs to happen automatically.
To do this we wrap every apiFetch call in a checker, that looks for 401 error which means that accessToken has gone out. When a apiFetch gets a 401,
we automatically send a refreshRequest to the backend with the refreshToken in localStorage. If the refresh does exist but is also invalid,
then the refresh process will reject and force user to login again for a new refreshToken. It all makes sense right?
We see a 401 so we send a req for a refresh meaning a new refreshToken but also a new accessToken. The old refreshToken gets revoked,
while we get a new one. This is to better track and monitor attackers. If a attacker gets hold of a users refreshToken and uses it for a new accessToken,
the user will get a error when their accessToken expieres because their refreshToken is now revoked. This makes us able to track the attackers and serves as an alarm for both the user and developers.
Also prevents users from easily using stolen refreshTokens as they might be revoked.
Further all refreshTokens are cleared when login or logout to prevent attackers from using old but still valid refreshToken for an extended period.
The attacker only has between an improper logout and the next login to use the refreshToken. For the more complicated part. We also have a queue for requests.
Imagine that several request all with invalid accessToken comes in at the same time, all 3 will start the refreshProcess but only the first one will succeed because by the time the other 2 start,
they are using a invalid refreshToken. To solve this we use a queue. We have a queue and a boolean to indicate if the refresh process is underway. If the refresh process is underway,
the other calls are placed into a queue. More specifically, the 2 methods are stored in queue, one that represents what the call wants to do and the other which represents what to do if refresh process fails.
These calls are in wait as promises to their caller to stall the request and. When the refresh process is done, a helper method activates which deals with all waiting requests.
it goes through the queue and activates either the success method or the fail method for them all. The success method in this case is to perform the orginal apiReq again.

TLDR:
✅ The problem — short tokens = security risk, long tokens = inconvenience.
✅ The solution — two tokens: short access + long refresh.
✅ Why store refresh tokens — need server-side revocation for long-lived tokens.
✅ Token rotation — detects stolen tokens, serves as alarm.
✅ Clear on login/logout — minimizes attacker window.
✅ Automatic refresh — 401 triggers refresh silently, user never notices.
✅ The queue — prevents multiple simultaneous refresh attempts.
✅ How the queue works — pre-saved methods, suspended promises, processQueue activates them.